What is a Container?

A Container is a lightweight, isolated environment that packages:

Application code
Runtime
Libraries
Dependencies
Configuration files

Containers ensure that applications run the same way on every system.

Characteristics
Lightweight
Portable
Fast startup
Shares the host operating system kernel
Uses fewer resources than virtual machines
Instance vs Container
Instance	Container
Virtual machine in the cloud	Lightweight application package
Includes its own operating system	Shares the host operating system
Uses more resources	Uses fewer resources
Slower startup	Faster startup
Runs multiple containers	Runs a single application or service
Cloud Deployment Example

A cloud server may have:

64 CPU Cores
128 GB RAM
16 TB Storage

On this server:

Multiple Instances can be created.
Each instance can run several Docker Containers.
Kubernetes manages and coordinates all the containers.
Key Terms
Term	Description
Cloud Server	Provides computing resources over the internet
Instance	A virtual machine running in the cloud
Docker	Tool used to create and run containers
Kubernetes	Platform used to manage multiple containers
Container	Lightweight package containing an application and its dependencies
CPU Cores	Number of processing units available
RAM	Temporary memory used during execution
Storage	Permanent space used to store data and applications
Day 7 Summary
Cloud servers provide resources such as CPU, RAM, and Storage.
Example server configuration: 64 Cores, 128 GB RAM, 16 TB Storage.
An Instance is a virtual machine that runs applications in the cloud.
Docker is used to create and run containers.
Kubernetes manages multiple Docker containers.
A Container packages an application with all its dependencies, ensuring consistent execution across environments.
Containers are lightweight, portable, and faster than virtual machines.
